### Why is "transient" expensive?

When a backend call fails, engineers classify the error as "transient" or "permanent" and wire retries to the transient bucket. The trap is that "transient" is a statement about time (this will get better) while the decision to retry requires a statement about state (did my previous attempt change anything).

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class TransientTrap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("'Transient' answers: Will this condition get better? YES.");
        System.out.println("Retry decision needs: Did my previous attempt succeed? UNKNOWN.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("These are TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS.");
        System.out.println("Conflating them is how duplicate payments happen.");
    }
}
```

```text
'Transient' answers: Will this condition get better? YES.
Retry decision needs: Did my previous attempt succeed? UNKNOWN.

These are TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS.
Conflating them is how duplicate payments happen.
```

### What are the four realities behind one timeout?

A single SocketTimeoutException hides four distinct states, and the client cannot tell which one occurred.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TimeoutRealities {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Rajesh calls: transfer 50,000 to Priya. Timeout occurs.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Reality A: Request NEVER ARRIVED. Retry is safe.");
        System.out.println("Reality B: Ledger is STILL PROCESSING. Retry creates duplicate.");
        System.out.println("Reality C: Transfer COMPLETED, ack lost. Retry creates duplicate.");
        System.out.println("Reality D: System OVERLOADED. Retry makes it worse.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Client sees ONE exception: SocketTimeoutException");
        System.out.println("Client CANNOT distinguish which reality occurred.");
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh calls: transfer 50,000 to Priya. Timeout occurs.

Reality A: Request NEVER ARRIVED. Retry is safe.
Reality B: Ledger is STILL PROCESSING. Retry creates duplicate.
Reality C: Transfer COMPLETED, ack lost. Retry creates duplicate.
Reality D: System OVERLOADED. Retry makes it worse.

Client sees ONE exception: SocketTimeoutException
Client CANNOT distinguish which reality occurred.
```

### How does idempotency make retries safe?

An idempotency key stays stable across retries. The provider records the key and treats any replay as a no-op, returning the original result.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class IdempotencyExample {

    private static final Map<String, String> processedKeys = new HashMap<>();

    static String settle(String idempotencyKey, String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        if (processedKeys.containsKey(idempotencyKey)) {
            return "Already processed: " + processedKeys.get(idempotencyKey);
        }
        // Process the transfer
        String result = "Transferred " + amount + " from " + from + " to " + to;
        processedKeys.put(idempotencyKey, result);
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String key = "TRF-20240115-001";
        System.out.println("First call: " + settle(key, "SAV2001", "SAV3002",
                new BigDecimal("50000.00")));
        System.out.println("Retry call: " + settle(key, "SAV2001", "SAV3002",
                new BigDecimal("50000.00")));
    }
}
```

```text
First call: Transferred 50000.00 from SAV2001 to SAV3002
Retry call: Already processed: Transferred 50000.00 from SAV2001 to SAV3002
```

### When should you use a status check instead of a blind retry?

For non-idempotent, high-value operations, a timeout should trigger a status check: ask the provider what happened, then act on the answer.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class StatusCheckOnTimeout {

    enum TransferState { NOT_FOUND, IN_PROGRESS, SETTLED, FAILED }

    static TransferState checkStatus(String idempotencyKey) {
        // Ask the ledger: what happened to this key?
        return TransferState.SETTLED; // simulated
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String key = "TRF-20240115-001";

        // Timeout occurred. Instead of blind retry:
        TransferState state = checkStatus(key);
        switch (state) {
            case NOT_FOUND -> System.out.println("Never received. Safe to resend.");
            case IN_PROGRESS -> System.out.println("Still processing. Wait and check again.");
            case SETTLED -> System.out.println("Already settled. Record success, do NOT resend.");
            case FAILED -> System.out.println("Failed. Safe to resend with same key.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Already settled. Record success, do NOT resend.
```

### What is wrong with blind retry after timeout?

Without idempotency, a blind retry can double the financial effect.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BlindRetryDanger {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal amount = new BigDecimal("50000.00");

        // WRONG: no idempotency key, blind retry
        System.out.println("Call 1: transfer " + amount + " (timed out, but actually completed)");
        System.out.println("Retry:  transfer " + amount + " (no idempotency → processes AGAIN)");
        System.out.println("Result: Priya receives " + amount.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(2)));
        System.out.println("Rajesh lost 1,00,000 instead of 50,000. Double payment.");
    }
}
```

```text
Call 1: transfer 50000.00 (timed out, but actually completed)
Retry:  transfer 50000.00 (no idempotency → processes AGAIN)
Result: Priya receives 100000.00
Rajesh lost 1,00,000 instead of 50,000. Double payment.
```

### Why is retrying into an overloaded system unwise even if idempotent?

Even when the operation is idempotent and retrying is logically correct, retrying into an overloaded dependency adds the exact traffic causing the failure.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class OverloadedRetry {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Idempotent: YES (retry is correct)");
        System.out.println("Dependency status: OVERLOADED");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Retry is CORRECT but UNWISE:");
        System.out.println("  Adding more traffic to an overloaded system makes it worse.");
        System.out.println("  Need: backoff, jitter, retry budget, circuit breaker.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("'Transient + idempotent' = retry is CORRECT.");
        System.out.println("It does NOT mean retry is WISE right now.");
    }
}
```

```text
Idempotent: YES (retry is correct)
Dependency status: OVERLOADED

Retry is CORRECT but UNWISE:
  Adding more traffic to an overloaded system makes it worse.
  Need: backoff, jitter, retry budget, circuit breaker.

'Transient + idempotent' = retry is CORRECT.
It does NOT mean retry is WISE right now.
```

### How do you compare the approaches?

| Approach | When Timeout Occurs | Risk |
|---|---|---|
| Blind retry (no key) | Resend without dedup | Double payment |
| Idempotent retry | Resend with stable key | Safe (deduplicated) but may add load |
| Status check then act | Query state, decide | Safest: no duplicate, no wasted work |
| Status check + idempotent retry | Check first, resend with key if needed | Best of both worlds |

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you generate a stable idempotency key for retries?
- What is the difference between at-most-once, at-least-once, and exactly-once delivery?
- How does a reconciliation job catch timeouts that were never resolved?
- Why should "unknown" be a first-class outcome in your system?
